"data: Hello world"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/About-Command-Line-Tools.rst:90
msgid "ROS 2 Daemon: Background Discovery Service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/About-Command-Line-Tools.rst:92
msgid ""
"ROS 2 uses a distributed discovery process for nodes to connect to each "
"other. As this process purposefully does not use a centralized discovery "
"mechanism, it can take time for ROS nodes to discover all other participants "
"in the ROS graph. To address this, ROS 2 runs a background daemon process "
"that maintains information about the ROS graph to provide faster responses "
"to queries, such as the list of node names."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/About-Command-Line-Tools.rst:96
msgid ""
"The ROS 2 daemon is automatically started when you first use command-line "
"tools like ``ros2 node list``, ``ros2 topic list``, or other introspection "
"commands. If no daemon is running, these tools will instantiate a new daemon "
"process in the background before executing the requested command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/About-Command-Line-Tools.rst:99
msgid ""
"The daemon communicates using the localhost network interface (127.0.0.1) "
"and uses the :doc:`ROS_DOMAIN_ID <../../ROS-Framework/nodes/About-Domain-"
"ID>` environment variable as a port number offset. This means that if you "
"want to control a specific daemon instance (for example, using ``ros2 daemon "
"stop``), you must ensure that your :doc:`ROS_DOMAIN_ID <../../ROS-Framework/"
"nodes/About-Domain-ID>` matches the domain ID used by that daemon. "
"Different :doc:`ROS_DOMAIN_ID <../../ROS-Framework/nodes/About-Domain-ID>` "
"values will result in separate daemon instances running on different ports."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/About-Command-Line-Tools.rst:103
msgid ""
"You can run ``ros2 daemon --help`` for more options for interacting with the "
"daemon, including commands to start, stop, or check the status of the daemon "
"process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/About-Command-Line-Tools.rst:106
msgid "Running the Daemon in the Foreground"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/About-Command-Line-Tools.rst:108
msgid ""
"For debugging purposes, it can be useful to run the ROS 2 daemon in the "
"foreground so that its output is printed directly to stdout and stderr. This "
"can be done using the ``_ros2_daemon`` command, which is the entry point for "
"the daemon process itself:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/About-Command-Line-Tools.rst:111
msgid "$ _ros2_daemon --ros-domain-id 0 --rmw-implementation rmw_fastrtps_cpp"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/About-Command-Line-Tools.rst:115
msgid ""
"This will start the daemon without daemonizing, allowing you to observe all "
"discovery activity and XML-RPC requests in real time. Replace ``--ros-domain-"
"id`` and ``--rmw-implementation`` values with those appropriate for your "
"setup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/About-Command-Line-Tools.rst:120
msgid ""
"Make sure to stop any existing daemon instance (``ros2 daemon stop``) before "
"starting one in the foreground to avoid port conflicts."
msgstr ""

